From d1941263eead9c72d945a65d29c0334a60ad6f4d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kayori <thatzokay@proton.me>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 23:30:36 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 29/30] grate: dual-source blending
 (GL_EXT_blend_func_extended) in two passes

Expose max_dual_source_render_targets = 1 (the assignment sat inside a
commented-out block and never took effect) and implement the extension.

The epilogue cannot hold src0, src1 and the destination at once: a
per-pixel value in a global register does not survive the destination read
and R0-R3 hold eight FX10 values. So a dual-source draw is drawn in up to
two passes, each a single-source blend that the existing epilogue handles
(fp/blend.h):

  dst pass:  colour = src1,            blend ZERO, Fd'      -> dst * Fd
  src pass:  colour = src0 * Fs(src1), blend ONE, ONE       -> + src0 * Fs

where Fd' is Fd written with the colour (SRC1_COLOR becomes SRC_COLOR).
A factor that is ONE (dst) or ZERO (src) skips its pass. This is exact as
long as the fragments of one draw do not overlap; fine for text. Supported:
equations ADD, factors ZERO, ONE and the four SRC1 ones.

The fragment compiler grew output modes (enum grate_fp_output_mode): store
output 0 as written, output 1, or output 0 times a factor made of output 1.
grate_compile_fs() is the old body of grate_create_fs_state() so a pass can
compile the same tokens differently, and the variants are cached on the
program (grate_fragment_dual_variant()). With a single output stored the
dead code of the other output disappears before allocation.

grate_draw_vbo() draws the passes by calling itself once per pass with
the pass's program and blend key. Epitaph's text shader (colour and plain
glyphs) renders exactly.
